    "turn_idx": 2,
    "turn_text": "my gosh, let's talk about what I think the appropriate response would be for the United States. If we wanted to have tariffs that would advance our trade agenda, but also do positive things in other regards. One thing, John, I've been putting forward is something we call a foreign pollution fee. Right now, China doesn't enforce environmental laws, so it lowers their cost of manufacturing 20%. That attracts businesses to move their manufacturing overseas, which is to say their jobs. One thing I put is a kind of a tariff. It affected the tariff. If you ship something to us, not using normal environmental standards and letting the pollution go into the atmosphere and blow over to us, that we're going to put a fee roughly equal to the amount of money you're not spending to control that pollution. You do that and you start going to a country like China and you're taking a little bit of, you're bringing those jobs back. They no longer have a 20% lower cost of manufacturing. You're doing something positive for the environment. You're giving China less money to militarize. Ultimately, they may decide to address the pollution by buying U.S. natural gas instead of their coal, which improves our economy. Lastly, you're going to end up creating more jobs in the U.S. I do think their solutions for these things will strike the balance between everybody's concerns.",
